      "Frances\nYip\ndiscovery\nEMI",
      "Frances Yip.\nA top artist today in Hong Kong, Frances was discovered by an\nItalian priest and professional tutor, under whose careful guidance\nthe rising star's talents developed. A veteran recording artist (more than\n70 singles and albums), Frances is well known to Hong Kong's T.V.\naudiences. In 1972 and 1973 she was voted most popular singer on T.V.\nand has topped the Chinese hit parade with several of her songs.\nA magnetic ability to capture an audience plus her strong but sensitive\ninterpretations, make Frances' style unique. On this \"discovery\"\nalbum she sings eleven songs of Asia in nine languages.\nts the theme of this album by Frances Yip - each song from\nnd fly away to \"discovery\"\nrevered folk song from Korea is handed down fro\nd Prix du disk for 1972. Its sad refrain of a\n\"Dahil Sa lyo\" (Philippines)\n'Rase Sayang Eh\" (Malaysia)\nJust one of many versions of an old, often-sung folk tune in whic\nsingers coin ohrases of their own to suit their individual mood\nSInE\nKondon Hong Kong one Cocial\nr (music by Ted Orley, word by Doug Bailey)\nold and very new, with an individual style and beat all its or\nhis album by Prince Blame Yuan\nVietnam\ntreatment typical of Vietnam toda\nngawan Solo River in Java as it fl\nthrough time and the lives of a million people\n\"Di Tenjong Katong\" (Singapore)\nanjong Katong - literally \"at the cape\" is a young and carefr\nlk song about this popular recreation spot on the island\n\"Waltzing Matil da\" (Australia\n* national folk song, It dates back to early",
